  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Total federal contract obligations in Louisiana are $4.7B for FY2026 to date, across 20,472 awards with an average award size of $227K. Total federal contract obligations in Louisiana fell 2.1% year over year. Department-level concentration is high: the Department of Homeland Security obligated $2.4B and the Department of Defense obligated $1.5B in the current fiscal year to date. Vendor concentration is also pronounced: BOLLINGER SHIPYARDS LOCKPORT, L.L.C. received $2.2B, while the next two vendors received $430M and $420M. Industry concentration centers on Ship Building and Repairing at $2.4B, followed by Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $464M and Facilities Support Services at $396M. Fiscal-year history shows obligations moving between $3.7B and $4.8B in recent years, with FY25 at $4.8B and FY26 at $4.7B. These figures are contract obligations and the current-year totals are partial, fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What changed year over year?",
        "body": "Total federal contract obligations in Louisiana fell 2.1% year over year. Award volume stands at 20,472, with an average award size of $227K. Year-to-year movement shows FY25 at $4.8B and FY26 at $4.7B, indicating a small downshift compared with the prior fiscal year. The decline comes despite large single-vendor and industry concentrations that keep totals elevated."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Where is the money concentrated?",
        "body": "The Department of Homeland Security is the largest buying agency, obligating $2.4B, followed by the Department of Defense at $1.5B. Ship Building and Repairing is the largest industry, with $2.4B in obligations. BOLLINGER SHIPYARDS LOCKPORT, L.L.C. is the largest vendor, receiving $2.2B, while other top vendors received $430M and $420M respectively. These few agencies, industries, and vendors account for a large share of the state total."
      }
    ]
